WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.890
Mel Gibson's biblical drama The Passion of
the Christ both divided and shocked audiences

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:08.780
nationwide after its release in 2004.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.579
Regardless, it received three Academy Award
nominations and remains the highest-grossing

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:15.599
R-rated film of all time.

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:19.670
On screen, The Passion of the Christ has its
fair share of bizarre moments — like the

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.590
part where Satan carries around a baby Voldemort.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:27.460
Mel Gibson purposely added those Uncanny Valley
moments just to catch audiences off guard,

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.160
saying: "That's what evil is about, taking
something that's good and twisting it a little

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.160
bit."

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.690
But as surreal as things can get during the
movie itself, there were even more bizarre

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:41.660
happenings behind the scenes — some of which
you might not even believe.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.340
Electrifying work

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:48.520
According to one study, lightning strikes
roughly 240,000 people per year.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:53.969
In other words, it's fairly rare — but in
2003, it happened three times on the set of

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:55.329
The Passion of the Christ.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:59.731
Lead actor Jim Caviezel claimed he was "lit
up like a Christmas tree" while filming the

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:01.760
Sermon on the Mount sequence of the film.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.300
"You got struck with lightning."

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.780
"I was struck by lightning."

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.000
"Light came right down from heaven and lit
me up.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.270
What people witnessed was an illumination
around my body and a fire on the right and

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.430
left side of my head."

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.140
But he wasn't the only person on the set to
get struck.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.140
Caviezel added:

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.810
"Five minutes after I got hit, Jon Mikalini,
an assistant, walks over and says 'Are you

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:22.810
okay?'

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:23.810
And then he got hit.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.670
The difference was that they saw the bolt
come down and hit Jon … All I felt was this

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:32.060
giant tremendous slap on my ears and a few
seconds of a pink, red static in front of

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.240
my eyes."

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.680
"Shazam!"

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.130
Unbelievably, that wasn't even the first time
the assistant director had been struck on

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:41.130
set.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.020
According to BBC News, lightning had already
struck his umbrella — luckily only causing

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.040
light burns on his fingertips.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.310
Whiplash

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.460
Because you can't actually beat your film's
star in real life, a hidden whipping post

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.780
was set up behind Caviezel while filming The
Passion of the Christ's notoriously bloody

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.159
scourging scene.

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:03.490
The problem was, one of the actors doing the
flogging didn't have the best aim.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.970
Caviezel recalled to Today:

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:09.660
"[His lash] just extended over the board and
hit me with such a velocity that I couldn't

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:10.660
breathe."

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.800
"I turned around and looked at the guy, and
I tell you, I may be playing Jesus, but I

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.830
felt like Satan at that moment … a couple
of expletives came out of my mouth."

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:22.060
Not that it helped — moments later, Caviezel
accidentally received another blow.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.480
Ouch.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:25.680
Cross purposes

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:30.410
Not only did Caviezel get struck by lightning
and accidentally whipped, he also dislocated

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.470
his shoulder while carrying the film's massive
crucifix.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:37.640
Caviezel's shoulder popping out of place was
just the start of his misery.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.720
Between the frigid cold and strong winds during
the crucifixion scenes, the actor also got

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.920
a lung infection and pneumonia.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.370
"My mouth was shaking uncontrollably.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.010
My arms and legs went numb - I was suffocating
on that cross."

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.010
But hey!

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.620
At least he didn't get migraines, right?

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.220
He got migraines

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.420
To add icing on top of Caviezel's cake of
pain, the actor regularly suffered severe

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.980
migraines from working day in and day out
with one eye cosmetically swollen shut.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.980
According to Today, Caviezel had to head in
to work at 2 AM every day in order to provide

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.870
enough time for the film's makeup artists
to cover him in cosmetic bruises, cuts, lashes,

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:15.870
and fake blood.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:20.510
They also had to arrange his crown of thorns,
and seal one eye shut — which wreaked havoc

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.040
on his depth perception.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.470
He told Fox News: "You are going to work every
day with only one eye functioning, which gives

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:27.470
you headaches."

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:31.540
And you thought eye strain from your office
job was bad.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.640
Dramatic conversion

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.450
Filming The Passion of the Christ had a pretty
profound effect on many people involved in

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.400
the production, and the film's set became
the site of some religious conversions.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.220
Father John Bartunek, who hung out on set,
told National Catholic Register, "Everyone

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.530
felt comfortable talking about issues of faith.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:52.790
Being a priest in the midst of that, I was
like a lightning rod for spiritual conversations."

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.760
Fortunately, in this case he didn't mean an
actual lightning rod.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.790
One actor who became a Christian on-set was
Luca Lionello, the actor who plays Judas.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.090
Bartunek explained:

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.560
"He asked for confession.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.040
Apparently he had been completely transformed
by the experience.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.930
He baptized his children, sanctified his marriage
and came back to the Church."

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:16.280
Naturally, not everyone who worked on the
set of The Passion of the Christ experienced

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.280
a religious epiphany — but most people were
affected in one way or another.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.049
Caviezel told The 700 Club:

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:26.590
"All the actors that worked on this film … it
will stay with them the rest of their lives.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:29.880
People will always come up to them, people
will always ask them about the movie they

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.280
were in, it will always be with them."

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.280
Career wrap

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.760
During the filming of The Passion of the Christ,
Caviezel had to endure a lot of pain and freak

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.560
accidents — but the bad luck didn't end
there.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.780
Caviezel has since claimed that he's been
blackballed by Hollywood for playing Jesus.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.280
In fact, he recalled Gibson trying to talk
him out of it because of the effect it would

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.290
have on his career.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.990
"A truly great remarkable man."

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:57.220
"Aw thank you very much Ron that's very kind
of you."

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.180
"Jesus Christ is here with me."

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:03.500
Still, Caviezel's career might be due for
a resurrection, with the actor set to reprise

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:07.070
his most famous role in Gibson's upcoming
sequel to The Passion.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:11.040
"It's a sequel to the Passion of the Christ,
is that true?"

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.430
"It's probably about three years off because
it's a big subject."

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:14.840
"Yeah, yeah, I read the book."

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.990
Caviezel also described the project to USA
Today, saying:

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.190
"I won't tell you how he's going to go about
it.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:23.780
But I'll tell you this much, the film he's
going to do is going to be the biggest film

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:24.780
in history.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:25.780
It's that good."

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.920
With so many bizarre happenings taking place
on the set of the original, we can only imagine

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:33.700
what's in store for the sequel — although
we're definitely hoping for fewer lightning

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:34.990
strikes.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:35.990
Thanks for watching!

